A||||10||recluse||throttle body coolent lines... purpose/removal?||||5||
Z||000000||recluse||11-16-2001||04:21 PM||||What is the purpose of the lines?  To help cool down the throttle body in order to get cooler air into the engine?  I will be pulling my intake off soon and would like to leave the lines off when i put it all back together. (One less thing to put back on) Are there any positives or negatives to leaving the lines off?  Where should i block them off?  On the throttle body as well as the rubber hoses?  <BR>Thanks for any help||||reg||5
Z||000001||ray b||11-16-2001||04:36 PM||||cold start and to prevent iceing of t/b<BR>old carbs used heat rizers in manafold that dragracers block off for more power.<BR>unsure if your are far enuff south to get good result in winter if removed,if in miami i'd say go for it.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||11-16-2001||04:38 PM||||yank em.. GT Bastard lives in SD and his cars ok.. mine and 4-5 others in the atl area have been fine for over 2 years.. it's free hp(well not much hp but every little bit helps).. and less weight.||||reg||1
Z||000003||recluse||11-16-2001||04:43 PM||||Thanks for the quick replys....  I dont think it gets cold enough here for anything to freeze up.  Rarely below 20 F.  Do I just block them off where the metal lines come out of the rubber hoses?  THanks alot ||||reg||1
Z||000004||avengador1||11-16-2001||04:56 PM||||You can block them off where they get their fluid. They come out of the thermostat housing (the tube with a radiator cap). Just get a short piece of rubber hose (make sure it can be used for this purpose, remember it will have hot fluid) and a couple of clamps to cap it off there. By removing them you will let the intake run cooler and will generate a little more H.P. because of the cooler denser air.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||11-16-2001||04:56 PM||||8" long piece of 3/8" coolant line.. follow the lines @ the coolant fill disconnect them at the fill tube and just make a loop with the 8" line.. remove the hard lines.. 15mm compression fitting 2 lines unscrew from the TB, throw hard lines far away. enjoy. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||recluse||11-16-2001||05:00 PM||||Thanks alot guys!  Sounds simple enough.||||reg||14
Z||000007||olliver||11-16-2001||09:30 PM||||Ok...I just have to ask!!!!!<P>Sure the theory of having removing these lines will result in a cooler intake.  But come on guys, will those lines really heat up the incoming air?<P>This is my opinion, and it's just that.<BR>First of all there is a MAT sensor which tells the computer the temperature of ambient air coming into the intake.  Now if the ecm calculates for this temperature, just how does it know how much the air has warmed up going through the throttle body?<P>I believe that at the speed of which the air is travelling through the intake it's not going to have a chance to warm up.  Because if it did then that MAT sensor would be incorrect.<P>Yes/NO?<P>Please correct me if i'm wrong.<P>Olliver||||reg||1
Z||000008||avengador1||11-17-2001||11:52 AM||||The difference in H.P. increase is so minimal, probably less than 1/2 H.P., but for those looking for every horse they can squeeze out, anything goes. Anyway this is a free (not counting coolant hose and clamps) modification. I haven't done it to mine and probably wont bother.||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||11-17-2001||12:10 PM||||Though I would wonder at even the gain of 1/2 horsepower, I took off the lines for purely asthetic purposes. While I had my engine apart I had a friend cut off and weld the tubes shut (or could one use the thermastat housing from the four cyl?) <P>It makes it easier to get to the distributor and I think it just cleans up the engine compartment. ||||reg||2
Z||000010||maryjane||11-17-2001||12:11 PM||||A note, probably insignificant. Ambient air does not have to be below freezing in order to produce below freezing temps in the throttle body or in a carb. Under the right conditions, (high humidity) you can produce frost on an air hose nozzle in the middle of summer. Not sure how this would relate in your application, but thought I'd pass it along. ||||reg||1
